<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Prespa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Prespa is a pair of lakes in the mountains of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Around 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Samuel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> made it a centre of his realm, building a basilica and residence on the island of Saint Achillius in Small Prespa Lake, where relics of the saint were brought from Larissa. The area remained a seat of Bulgarian power until the Byzantine conquest of 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-prespa.jpg" alt="Lake Prespa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fation Plaku · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liqeni_Prespes._Ishulli_Mali_Gradit._Korce._Albania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Prespa lies at the tripoint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, at an elevation of 853 metres, and the two lakes are the highest tectonic lakes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. The Great Prespa Lake covers 259 square kilometres, of which 176.3 belong to North Macedonia, 46.3 to Albania and 36.4 to Greece. The Little Prespa Lake to the south covers 46.8 square kilometres, mostly in Greece, with its westernmost tip of 4.3 in Albania. A 4-kilometre isthmus, 500 metres wide, on Greek territory separates them. It carries an embankment and a road to the village of Psarades, and a short canal links the lakes at its western side.</p><p>The Great Prespa sits in limestone country about 150 metres above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ohrid">Lake Ohrid</a>, which lies about 10 kilometres to the west. Its only outlets are underground channels in the karst, which emerge as springs feeding streams that run into Lake Ohrid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Classical times the region formed part of ancient Lynkestis, and the lakes were called Little and Great Brygeis. In the 10th century Tsar Samuel built a fortress and a church of St Achillius on the island of Agios Achillios in the Small Prespa Lake, on the Greek side. The largest island of the Great Prespa, Golem Grad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, and Mal Grad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, the site of a ruined 14th-century monastery of St Peter, are both uninhabited today.</p><p>The Greek part of the region was for many years underpopulated and militarily sensitive, and outsiders needed special permission to visit. It saw fierce fighting during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-civil-war">Greek Civil War</a>, and much of the local population then emigrated to escape poverty and political strife. In the 1970s the Albanian communist regime diverted the Devoll River, which fed the Little Prespa, to irrigate farmland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korce">Korçë</a>, which contributed to the shrinking of the lake's surface. On 17 June 2018 the Prespa Agreement, renaming the Republic of Macedonia as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, was signed at Psarades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The area holds three national parks: Galičica National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and Prespa National Park in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The largest town of the region is Resen in North Macedonia. In 2014 the Ohrid-Prespa Transboundary Biosphere Reserve between Albania and North Macedonia was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Network of Biosphere Reserves, and the area was declared a Transnational Park in 2000. Of the 11 native fish species of the lake, nine are endemic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Prespa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The youngest, Samuel, emerged as ruler, defeated Basil II at the Gates of Trajan in 986 and, ruling from <b>Prespa</b> and Ohrid, extended his power to Dyrrhachium, Thessaly and Duklja, taking the title of tsar around 997.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
